UNIT hash_ok
LANG js
PRIMARY-SHA256 a7fc3172f82e6f55fca50cd60860c3aa6a8917cada4354fb1d6a6db752da2f0c
SIG hash_ok(text: string | Uint8Array, want?: string | null): boolean
NOTE the reconstruction exports a plain function (named export hash_ok); the real code is verdict.hashOk(bytes, want).
ABI text=string (UTF-16 code units); bool=boolean; none=null. The pin may also arrive as undefined or be omitted.
MECHANISM check order is fixed and observable: (1) want === undefined or want === null -> true; (2) want not a string,
  or failing the pin test -> throw an Error; (3) text a string that is not well-formed (String.prototype.isWellFormed
  false) -> throw an Error; (4) return digest === want. A later step never runs before an earlier one.
MECHANISM digest (R3) = node:crypto createHash('sha256').update(text).digest('hex') — update on a string encodes it as
  UTF-8, and digest('hex') is already lowercase. No third-party package.
PINNED regex PIN = /^[0-9a-f]{64}$/ CITED R2 — no flags; JS `$` without the m flag matches only at the end of input,
  so a trailing "\n" fails the pin (unlike dialects whose `$` also matches before a final newline).
MECHANISM throw messages (informational; only the fact of a throw is graded): step 2 `hashOk: a pin is 64 lowercase
  hex digits (got <JSON.stringify(want) for a string, else typeof want>)`; step 3 `hashOk: text with a lone surrogate
  has no UTF-8 form of its own`.
ENVELOPE widening (js extension, verified by the js variant only):
  missing pin — want undefined or omitted is the same as null -> true, before text is touched.
  non-string pin — any other non-string want (0, false, true, 0n, an array even if it holds the right hex, an object)
  throws at step 2, whatever text is. Nothing is coerced.
  lone surrogates in text — a string with any unpaired surrogate code unit (including a reversed pair "\uDE00\uD83D")
  throws at step 3, but only once want has passed steps 1 and 2: with a null/undefined want it is true, with a non-pin
  want the pin error wins.
  byte input — a Uint8Array (or Buffer) text skips step 3 and is hashed as its raw bytes (invalid UTF-8 included).
  non-text text — a number, null, boolean, array or plain object as text: step 1 -> true and step 2 -> throws as usual;
  with a valid pin the digest call throws node's ERR_INVALID_ARG_TYPE TypeError (`The "data" argument must be of type
  string or an instance of Buffer, TypedArray, or DataView. Received …`).
DEPENDS-ON none
PROVENANCE rederive@bb8ded84fa50 cli/verdict.mts:109–119 (hashHex, hashOk).
